<h2>Random Matrix Theory — the Wigner Semicircle Law, the Gaussian Ensembles &amp; Tracy-Widom</h2>
<p>Random Matrix Theory (RMT) studies the eigenvalue statistics of matrices whose entries are random variables. For an n×n <strong>Wigner matrix</strong> — a symmetric (or Hermitian) random matrix with independent (up to symmetry) entries — the <strong>empirical spectral distribution</strong> μ_n = (1/n) Σ δ_{λ_i} converges to the <strong>semicircle law</strong> as n → ∞. The three <strong>Gaussian ensembles</strong> — GOE (β=1, real symmetric), GUE (β=2, complex Hermitian), GSE (β=4, quaternion self-dual) — share the joint eigenvalue density P(λ₁,…,λ_n) ∝ Π_{i&lt;j} |λ_i−λ_j|^β · exp(−β Σ λ_i²/4). The <strong>Tracy-Widom distribution</strong> governs the fluctuations of the largest eigenvalue at the edge, while the <strong>Marchenko-Pastur law</strong> describes the spectrum of sample covariance matrices. RMT finds applications across physics (quantum chaos, nuclear spectra), finance (covariance matrix of returns), statistics (high-dimensional PCA), and wireless communications.</p>
<div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"Random matrix theory is the statistical mechanics of eigenvalues."</div><div class="attribution">— Freeman Dyson</div></div>
<div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div>
<h2>📏 The Gaussian Ensembles and the Wigner Semicircle Law</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Wigner</div><div class="story-title">Wigner Matrices, the Semicircle Law, and the Stieltjes Transform</div><div class="story-text">A <strong>Wigner matrix</strong> X is an n×n symmetric (or Hermitian) random matrix with entries X_{ii} ~ N(0,2) and X_{ij} ~ N(0,1) for i &lt; j (i.i.d. up to symmetry). The <strong>empirical spectral distribution</strong> (ESD) is μ_n = (1/n) Σ_{i=1}^{n} δ_{λ_i}, where λ_i are the eigenvalues. <strong>Wigner's semicircle law</strong> (1955, 1958): as n → ∞, the ESD of X/√n converges weakly to ρ(x) = (1/(2π))√(4−x²) for |x| ≤ 2, and zero otherwise. The proof uses the <strong>Stieltjes transform</strong>: m_n(z) = (1/n) Tr(G_n(z)) where G_n(z) = (X/√n − zI)^{-1} is the resolvent. The Stieltjes transform of the limiting measure satisfies the <strong>self-consistent equation</strong>: m(z) = ∫ ρ(x)/(x−z) dx with limit −1/m(z) = m(z) + z. Solving gives m(z) = (−z + √(z²−4))/2, whose imaginary part recovers the semicircle density via the inversion formula ρ(x) = lim_{ε→0⁺} (1/π) Im m(x + iε). For the Gaussian Wigner matrix (β=2), the joint eigenvalue density is P(λ) ∝ exp(−½ Σ λ_i²) Π_{i&lt;j} |λ_i−λ_j|². The <strong>Coulomb gas analogy</strong>: eigenvalues behave like charged particles in 2D with logarithmic repulsion — the Vandermonde determinant Π_{i&lt;j} |λ_i−λ_j| encodes the repulsion, while the Gaussian factor exp(−β Σ λ_i²/4) provides a confining harmonic potential.</div><div class="story-moral">The Wigner semicircle law is the RMT analogue of the central limit theorem — the universal limiting spectral density for i.i.d. symmetric random matrices.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div>
<h2>🌍 The Gaussian Orthogonal Ensemble (GOE)</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">GOE</div><div class="story-title">GOE, GUE, GSE — Joint Densities, Kernels, and Spacing Distributions</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>Gaussian Orthogonal Ensemble (GOE)</strong> consists of n×n real symmetric matrices H with probability density P(H) ∝ exp(−Tr(H²)/4) dH. The <strong>joint eigenvalue density</strong> is P(λ₁,…,λ_n) = Z_n^{-1} exp(−¼ Σ λ_i²) Π_{i&lt;j} |λ_i−λ_j|. The normalization Z_n is computed via the <strong>Selberg integral</strong>. The eigenvalue density ρ_n(λ) = (1/n) K_n(λ,λ) where K_n(x,y) = Σ_{k=0}^{n−1} φ_k(x)φ_k(y) is the kernel of the <strong>Hermite polynomials</strong> (φ_k are the normalized Hermite functions). The <strong>Christoffel-Darboux formula</strong> gives a closed form: K_n(x,y) = √(n/2) (φ_n(x)φ_{n-1}(y)−φ_{n-1}(x)φ_n(y))/(x−y). The <strong>GUE (β=2)</strong> has the same structure with joint density P(λ) ∝ exp(−½ Σ λ_i²) Π_{i&lt;j} |λ_i−λ_j|² and the squared Hermite kernel. The <strong>GSE (β=4)</strong> involves quaternion self-dual matrices. The <strong>nearest-neighbor spacing distribution</strong>: in the bulk, with spacing s = (λ_{i+1}−λ_i)/D (mean spacing D), the <strong>Wigner surmise</strong> for β=1 gives P(s) = (πs/2)exp(−πs²/4). For β=2 (GUE): P(s) = (32s²/π²)exp(−4s²/π). For β=4 (GSE): P(s) = (2^{18}s⁴/3⁶π³)exp(−64s²/9π). These are excellent approximations to the exact ( Painlevé ) spacing distributions. The <strong>spectral rigidity</strong> Δ₃(L) and the <strong>number variance</strong> Σ²(L) measure long-range correlations — for RMT ensembles, Σ²(L) ~ (2/βπ²) log L + constant, exhibiting logarithmic repulsion (in contrast to Poisson statistics Σ²(L) ~ L for uncorrelated eigenvalues).</div><div class="story-moral">The Gaussian ensembles form a three-fold universality class (β = 1, 2, 4) distinguished by the symmetry type of the matrix entries, each with characteristic eigenvalue repulsion and spacing distributions.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div>
<h2>🧮 The Tracy-Widom Distribution</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Edge</div><div class="story-title">Largest Eigenvalue Fluctuations, Painlevé II, and the KPZ Equation</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>largest eigenvalue</strong> λ_max of an n×n GUE matrix, after centering and scaling by χ₁ = n^{2/3}(λ_max − 2), converges in distribution to the <strong>Tracy-Widom (TW) distribution</strong> F₂(t). The TW distribution is expressed in terms of the <strong>Painlevé II transcendent</strong>: F₂(t) = exp(−∫_t^∞ (x−t) q(x)² dx), where q is the unique solution of <strong>Painlevé II</strong>: q'' = tq + 2q³, with the boundary condition q(t) ∼ Ai(t) as t → ∞ (the <strong>Airy function</strong>). For GOE (β=1), F₁(t) = exp(−½∫_t^∞ q(x) (∫_x^∞ q(y)² dy) dx). For GSE (β=4), F₄(√2 t) = (F₂(t))² · (exp(∫_t^∞ q(x) dx) + exp(−∫_t^∞ q(x) dx))/2. The TW distribution is <strong>universal</strong> — it describes the fluctuations of the largest eigenvalue not only for Gaussian ensembles but for a broad class of random matrices with i.i.d. entries (the Tracy-Widom universality). Remarkably, the same distribution appears in the <strong>1D Kardar-Parisi-Zhang (KPZ) equation</strong> height fluctuations (the Baik-Rains distribution for flat initial conditions and the GUE Tracy-Widom for curved). The <strong>Baik-Ben Arous-Péché (BBP) phase transition</strong>: if a rank-1 perturbation A = M + θ vv^T is added to a GUE matrix M, the largest eigenvalue transitions from the TW distribution (when θ &lt; 1, subcritical) to a Gaussian distribution (when θ &gt; 1, supercritical) at the critical signal-to-noise ratio θ_c = 1 — a sharp detectability threshold in spiked models.</div><div class="story-moral">The Tracy-Widom distribution is the universal law of edge eigenvalue fluctuations in random matrix theory, connecting to Painlevé transcendents and the KPZ universality class.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div>
<h2>🔥 The Marchenko-Pastur Law and Applications</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">MP Law</div><div class="story-title">Sample Covariance Matrices, Johnstone's Theorem, and RMT in Data Science</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>sample covariance matrix</strong> S = (1/n) XX^T where X is a p×n matrix with i.i.d. entries of mean 0 and variance σ². As p,n → ∞ with p/n → γ &gt; 0, the empirical spectral distribution of S converges to the <strong>Marchenko-Pastur (MP) law</strong>: ρ(x) = (1/(2πσ²γx))√((b−x)(x−a)) for a ≤ x ≤ b where a,b = σ²(1±√γ)², plus a point mass at 0 if γ &gt; 1. The <strong>Stieltjes transform</strong> satisfies m(z) = (1−γ)/z − (1/z)·1/(1+γσ²·m(z)). Applications: in <strong>high-dimensional PCA</strong>, sample eigenvalues are biased (the Baik-Jin statistics) — the top eigenvalue undergoes a phase transition (the BBP transition) depending on the signal strength. The <strong>Tracy-Widom distribution for the largest eigenvalue of the sample covariance matrix</strong> (<strong>Johnstone's theorem</strong>, 2001): for real data, the largest eigenvalue follows the TW₁ distribution (GOE edge); for complex data, the TW₂ distribution (GUE edge). The <strong>Benaych-Georges and Nadakuditi theory of low-rank perturbations</strong> provides a complete framework for detecting signals in noisy matrices. In <strong>finance</strong>, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix of stock returns separate into a bulk (described by the MP law, corresponding to noise) and a few large eigenvalues (corresponding to market modes and sectors). In <strong>quantum chaos</strong>, the spectral statistics of chaotic quantum systems (e.g., the Riemann zeroes, nuclear energy levels) follow the GOE/GUE predictions — the <strong>spectral rigidity</strong> Δ₃(L) and the <strong>number variance</strong> Σ²(L) of the Riemann zeta function zeros match GUE predictions. <strong>Sparse random matrices</strong> (e.g., adjacency matrices of Erdős-Rényi graphs) exhibit a transition from a semicircle-like bulk to localized eigenvectors (the Anderson transition on random graphs).</div><div class="story-moral">The Marchenko-Pastur law and the Tracy-Widom edge statistics provide the fundamental tools for random matrix inference in high-dimensional statistics, finance, and quantum physics.</div></div>
